// Day 9 question pool: Domain 2, Design Resilient Architectures (Task 2.1 loose coupling, Task 2.2 HA/DR). Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q09-001", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"An e-commerce company sends order lifecycle updates (created, paid, shipped, cancelled) to Amazon SQS for processing by a fleet of consumers. Updates for the same order must be processed exactly once and in the order they were sent. Updates for different orders are independent, and the company wants many consumers to work on different orders in parallel. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Use an SQS Standard queue and have consumers sort each order's updates by timestamp before processing them.",
    "Use an SQS FIFO queue with a single message group ID for all messages and a deduplication ID per update.",
    "Use an SQS FIFO queue with the order ID as the message group ID and a deduplication ID per update.",
    "Use an SQS Standard queue with long polling and a visibility timeout longer than the processing time."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Standard queues give best-effort ordering and at-least-once delivery, so duplicates still occur and consumers would need custom sorting and deduplication logic.",
    "This preserves order and removes duplicates, but a message group is worked on by only one consumer at a time, so every order would be serialized and processing could not scale out.",
    "FIFO queues preserve order and deduplicate within each message group. Using the order ID as the group ID keeps each order in sequence while different orders are processed in parallel by many consumers.",
    "Long polling and a suitable visibility timeout reduce empty receives and redeliveries, but a Standard queue still provides neither strict ordering nor exactly-once processing."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-002", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A media company uses an SQS Standard queue with default settings to distribute video transcoding jobs to EC2 worker instances. Each job takes 4–6 minutes, and a worker deletes the message only after its transcode finishes. Operators notice that many videos are transcoded two or three times, although the workers log no errors. What should a solutions architect do to resolve this issue?",
 o:["Increase the visibility timeout to exceed the longest expected job duration.",
    "Set the queue's receive message wait time to 20 seconds to enable long polling.",
    "Create an SQS FIFO queue with content-based deduplication and move the workers to it.",
    "Set a delivery delay of 6 minutes on the queue so that each job is delivered only once."],
 a:[0],
 x:["The default visibility timeout is 30 seconds, so the message reappears while the first worker is still transcoding and another worker picks it up. A timeout above the maximum job time (or extending it with ChangeMessageVisibility) stops these redeliveries.",
    "Long polling reduces empty ReceiveMessage responses and cost; it does not stop a message from becoming visible again while a worker is still processing it.",
    "FIFO deduplication only drops duplicate sends within a 5-minute window. A message whose visibility timeout expires is still redelivered, so the root cause remains.",
    "A delivery delay only hides a message when it is first sent. Once it is received, the 30-second visibility timeout still expires mid-job and causes repeat processing."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-003", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A payments platform processes transactions from an SQS Standard queue with a fleet of containers. Occasionally a malformed message makes the consumer fail; the message returns to the queue and is retried over and over until it expires after 4 days, wasting capacity and flooding the logs. The team wants such messages set aside automatically after three failed attempts so they can be inspected and replayed later, without changing the consumer code. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Reduce the queue's message retention period to 1 hour so that failing messages expire much sooner.",
    "Increase the visibility timeout to 12 hours so that failing messages are retried less often.",
    "Create a second queue and update the consumer to send any message that fails to that queue.",
    "Create a dead-letter queue and a redrive policy on the source queue with a maxReceiveCount of 3."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Shorter retention deletes failing messages along with any valid backlog older than an hour, so data is lost and nothing is kept for inspection.",
    "A longer visibility timeout slows the retry loop but never ends it, and it also delays legitimate retries after transient failures.",
    "This works but requires changing the consumer code, which the team wants to avoid; a redrive policy moves the messages without any code.",
    "After a message has been received three times without being deleted, SQS moves it to the dead-letter queue. The team can inspect it there and use DLQ redrive to send it back to the source queue after a fix."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-004", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"A logistics company's tracking workers poll an SQS queue in a tight loop. Traffic is bursty, and the queue is often empty for long periods. The SQS bill and worker CPU usage are higher than expected because most ReceiveMessage calls return no messages. Which change reduces empty responses and cost with the least effort?",
 o:["Set a delivery delay of 15 minutes on the queue to batch up messages before workers see them.",
    "Set the queue's <code>ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds</code> attribute to 20 to enable long polling.",
    "Increase the queue's visibility timeout so that each received message stays hidden for longer.",
    "Request up to 10 messages per ReceiveMessage call while keeping short polling on the queue."],
 a:[1],
 x:["A delivery delay holds every new message back, adding latency to tracking updates, and workers still get empty responses while they poll.",
    "With long polling, ReceiveMessage waits up to 20 seconds for a message to arrive before returning, which removes most empty responses and the request charges and CPU spent on them. It is a single queue attribute change.",
    "Visibility timeout controls how long a received message stays hidden; it has no effect on how often an empty queue is polled.",
    "Batching helps when messages are present, but with short polling an empty queue still returns immediately, so the tight loop keeps making empty calls."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-005", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A healthcare analytics company runs document-processing workers on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. The workers pull jobs from an SQS queue whose arrival rate is unpredictable. Each instance processes about 20 messages per minute, and no job should wait more than about 5 minutes. Scaling on CPU utilization works poorly because CPU stays low while the workers wait on I/O. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take to scale the fleet correctly? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create a predictive scaling policy for the Auto Scaling group that is based on CPU utilization.",
    "Publish a custom CloudWatch metric for backlog per instance: visible messages divided by in-service instances.",
    "Create scheduled scaling actions that add instances during business hours and remove them at night.",
    "Create a target tracking scaling policy on the backlog-per-instance metric with a target value of 100.",
    "Create a target tracking scaling policy on the ALBRequestCountPerTarget metric for the group."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Predictive scaling forecasts from history, and CPU is the metric that already fails to reflect the real load, so it would still scale on the wrong signal.",
    "Queue depth alone ignores fleet size. ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible divided by the number of InService instances shows how much work each instance has waiting, which maps directly to job wait time (it can also be calculated with metric math inside the policy instead of being published).",
    "Arrivals are unpredictable, so fixed schedules would either leave jobs waiting during unexpected bursts or keep idle instances running.",
    "The acceptable backlog per instance is 20 messages per minute × 5 minutes = 100. Target tracking adds or removes instances to hold each instance's backlog near that value.",
    "The workers pull from SQS and are not behind a load balancer, so there are no ALB requests to scale on."],
 ref:["d9e2","d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q09-006", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SNS","SQS"],
 q:"A retail company's order service must notify three downstream systems (inventory, billing, and analytics) every time an order is placed. Each system is owned by a different team, consumes at its own pace, and must not miss events even if it is offline for several hours of maintenance. The order service should not need to know which consumers exist. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Publish each order event once to an Amazon SNS standard topic.",
    "Create one SQS queue that all three systems poll for new order events.",
    "Create an SQS queue for each system and subscribe each queue to the topic.",
    "Subscribe each system's HTTPS endpoint directly to the SNS topic.",
    "Have the order service send every event to each system's own SQS queue."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["The producer publishes once and SNS delivers a copy to every subscriber, so adding or removing consumers needs no change to the order service.",
    "With one shared queue, each message is received and deleted by only one consumer, so the three systems would compete for events instead of each getting a copy.",
    "Each queue receives its own copy of every event and holds it durably (retention up to 14 days), so a system that is offline for hours catches up when it returns.",
    "SNS retries HTTPS deliveries only for a limited time, so an endpoint that is down for hours can lose events, and there is no buffer to consume at its own pace.",
    "The producer would have to know and maintain the list of consumer queues, which is exactly the tight coupling the company wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q09-007", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SNS"],
 q:"A ride-sharing company publishes all trip events (requested, started, completed, cancelled) to a single SNS topic that fans out to several SQS queues. The fraud team's consumer needs only cancelled trips with a fare above a set amount, and today it discards more than 95% of the messages it receives. The company wants to stop delivering unneeded messages to the fraud queue without changing the publisher or adding topics. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Pass the event type in the MessageAttributeNames parameter when the fraud consumer calls ReceiveMessage.",
    "Insert a Lambda function between the topic and the fraud queue that forwards only the matching events.",
    "Add a filter policy to the fraud queue's subscription that matches cancelled trips above the fare amount.",
    "Create a second topic for cancelled trips and have the publisher send those events to both of the topics."],
 a:[2],
 x:["MessageAttributeNames only chooses which attributes are returned with each message; SQS does not filter on receive, so every message is still delivered to the queue.",
    "This works but adds a function to build, run, and pay for, when SNS can filter natively at the subscription.",
    "SNS subscription filter policies can match message attributes or the message body, including numeric ranges, so only cancelled trips above the threshold reach the fraud queue. Other subscriptions are unaffected.",
    "This changes the publisher and adds a topic, both of which the company wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q09-008", day:9, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"A customer-support company uses a third-party SaaS helpdesk that is an Amazon EventBridge partner. Each new high-priority ticket must start an AWS Step Functions workflow and also be archived to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose. The company wants no polling code and no servers to manage. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a rule on the default event bus that matches the helpdesk's ticket events and targets both the state machine and the Firehose stream.",
    "Associate the partner event source with an event bus, and add a rule on that bus that matches high-priority tickets and targets both resources.",
    "Use EventBridge Scheduler to run a Lambda function every minute that calls the helpdesk API and starts the workflow for new tickets.",
    "Point the helpdesk webhook at an SNS topic, and subscribe both the state machine and the Firehose stream to that topic."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Partner events are delivered to the partner event bus associated with the event source, not to the default event bus, so this rule would never match.",
    "Once the partner event source is associated with its event bus, one rule with an event pattern on priority can send each matching event to several targets, including a Step Functions state machine and a Firehose stream, with no code.",
    "This is polling code that the company wants to avoid, and it adds up to a minute of delay and depends on the helpdesk API's limits.",
    "A state machine is not among the SNS subscription protocols (HTTP/S, email, SMS, SQS, Lambda, Firehose, mobile push), and a webhook cannot publish to SNS without signed AWS requests, which means writing and hosting custom code."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q09-009", day:9, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge","Lambda"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs 40 hourly and nightly maintenance jobs as cron entries on a single EC2 instance. Each job calls an internal API and finishes in under 2 minutes. When the instance failed last month, several billing jobs silently did not run. The company wants a highly available, serverless replacement with minimal operational overhead and automatic retries for failed runs. Which solution should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Run the same cron entries on two EC2 instances in different Availability Zones.",
    "Put the instance in an Auto Scaling group with a minimum and maximum of one across two AZs.",
    "Use a Step Functions Express workflow that loops with Wait states until each job's next scheduled run time arrives.",
    "Create EventBridge Scheduler schedules that invoke Lambda functions, with retries and a dead-letter queue."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Both instances would run every job, causing duplicate billing runs, and the company would still be patching and managing servers.",
    "Auto Scaling replaces a failed instance, but jobs scheduled during the replacement are missed, and there is still a server to manage.",
    "Express workflows run for at most 5 minutes, so they cannot wait for hourly or nightly schedules.",
    "EventBridge Scheduler is a serverless, managed scheduler with cron and rate expressions, configurable retry policies, and a dead-letter queue for runs that still fail, so the company has no scheduling server to fail or patch. Lambda runs each short job without servers. (EventBridge scheduled rules also run on a schedule, but AWS labels them legacy and recommends Scheduler.)"],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"scheduler"},

{id:"q09-010", day:9, dom:2, tags:["EventBridge"],
 q:"A company uses AWS Organizations with 60 member accounts. The security team wants security group changes and root user sign-in events from every account delivered in near real time to a central security account, where one set of EventBridge rules routes them to an incident-response workflow. Member account teams must not be able to read events from other accounts. Which design meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Create a custom event bus in the security account whose resource policy lets the organization put events, and add a rule in each member account that targets it.",
    "Create a rule on the security account's default event bus that matches security events from every member account's default event bus.",
    "Create an SNS topic in every member account and subscribe the security account's incident-response workflow to each of the 60 topics.",
    "Run a Lambda function in the security account that assumes a role in each member account every 5 minutes and queries CloudTrail event history."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A rule in each account targets the central bus, and the bus policy with an aws:PrincipalOrgID condition accepts events only from the organization. Member accounts can send but not read, and the member rules can be rolled out with CloudFormation StackSets.",
    "A rule matches only events that arrive on its own event bus; it cannot read events from buses in other accounts, so each member account must send its events to the central bus.",
    "Managing 60 topics and subscriptions is heavy, and the routing logic would be scattered instead of living in one set of EventBridge rules.",
    "Polling every 5 minutes is not near real time, and maintaining cross-account roles and query code adds operational overhead."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q09-011", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Step Functions"],
 q:"An insurance company is replacing a monolithic claims process with microservices. Each claim must call a fraud-check service, a payment service, and a notification service in sequence. The fraud-check service is sometimes throttled and should be retried with increasing delays. If the payment step fails permanently, a refund-and-notify compensation step must run. The company wants this flow defined and visible outside the application code. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Chain the services with SQS queues and attach a dead-letter queue to each of the queues.",
    "Write an orchestrator Lambda function that calls each service and sleeps between retry attempts.",
    "Model the process as a Step Functions state machine with a Retry on the fraud-check task that uses a backoff rate.",
    "Publish each claim to an SNS topic that all three services subscribe to.",
    "Add a Catch on the payment task in the state machine that transitions to a compensation state."],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Queues decouple the steps, but retries happen at a fixed visibility timeout rather than increasing delays, compensation must be hand-coded, and the end-to-end flow is not visible in one place.",
    "Retry and compensation logic stays buried in code, the function pays for time spent sleeping, and an invocation of a standard Lambda function cannot run longer than 15 minutes.",
    "Step Functions Retry fields support IntervalSeconds, MaxAttempts, and BackoffRate for exponential backoff on specific errors, and the workflow graph shows every step and its status.",
    "SNS fan-out delivers to all three services at once, so the steps would run in parallel instead of in the required sequence, with no retry or compensation control.",
    "A Catch field routes specific errors, once retries are exhausted, to another state, which is how a saga-style compensation step such as refund-and-notify is triggered."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q09-012", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Step Functions"],
 q:"A mortgage lender orchestrates loan approvals with AWS Step Functions. After automated checks, the workflow must pause until an underwriter approves or rejects the application in an internal web portal, which can take up to 10 days. The workflow must resume as soon as the decision is made, without polling and without paying for idle compute while it waits. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Use an Express workflow with a task state that waits for the portal to call back with the underwriter's decision, however long it takes.",
    "Use a Standard workflow task with the .waitForTaskToken pattern; the portal returns the token with SendTaskSuccess or SendTaskFailure.",
    "Add a Wait state of 10 days followed by a Lambda function that reads the decision from a database.",
    "Use an activity task and run a worker on an EC2 instance that calls GetActivityTask in a loop until an underwriter's decision exists."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Express workflows have a maximum duration of 5 minutes and do not support the callback pattern, so they cannot wait days for a human decision.",
    "The callback pattern hands a task token to the portal (for example through SQS or a Lambda function) and the execution pauses at no compute cost. Standard workflows can run for up to a year, and the token call resumes the workflow immediately.",
    "The workflow would always wait the full 10 days even if the underwriter decides within an hour, so it would not resume as soon as the decision is made.",
    "This keeps an EC2 worker running and polling for days, which is exactly the idle compute and polling the lender wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"stepfunctions"},

{id:"q09-013", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"An IoT company uses Amazon S3 event notifications to invoke a Lambda function asynchronously for each uploaded sensor file. When processing still fails after Lambda's built-in retries, the operations team needs a record containing the original event, the invocation context such as the number of attempts, and the function's full error response, so it can investigate and reprocess. The team does not want to add error-handling code to the function. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure the S3 bucket to send event notifications to an SNS topic instead of invoking Lambda.",
    "Increase the function's maximum retry attempts and maximum event age to their highest values.",
    "Configure an SQS dead-letter queue on the function for events that fail asynchronous processing.",
    "Configure an SQS on-failure destination for the function's asynchronous invocations."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Changing the notification target adds a hop but captures none of Lambda's failure details; failures would still be lost unless more components and code were added.",
    "More retries and a longer event age only delay the failure; events that still fail are discarded without any record.",
    "A function dead-letter queue receives only the event payload with brief error attributes; it lacks the invocation context and full response that the team needs.",
    "An on-failure destination receives an invocation record with the request payload, the request context (including the attempt count and failure condition), and the response payload with the error details, with no code changes."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q09-014", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Lambda"],
 q:"A gaming company runs dozens of Lambda functions in one AWS account. During a marketing event, a batch analytics function scaled out sharply and used almost all of the account's concurrency, so the player-login function, invoked through API Gateway, was throttled. The login function never needs more than 500 concurrent executions, but those 500 must always be available regardless of what other functions do. Which solution meets this requirement most cost-effectively?",
 o:["Configure provisioned concurrency of 500 on the login function.",
    "Request an increase to the account's concurrent executions quota.",
    "Configure reserved concurrency of 500 on the login function.",
    "Set API Gateway stage throttling for the login API to 500 requests per second."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Provisioned concurrency pre-initializes environments to remove cold starts and is billed for as long as it is configured, which costs more than needed just to guarantee capacity.",
    "A higher quota adds total concurrency, but nothing stops the analytics function from consuming it again, so the login function is still unprotected.",
    "Reserved concurrency sets aside 500 executions from the account pool for the login function alone, so no other function can take them. It has no additional charge.",
    "Throttling the login API limits the login function itself; it does nothing to stop the analytics function from exhausting account concurrency."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q09-015", day:9, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway"],
 q:"A weather data company exposes a REST API through Amazon API Gateway, backed by Lambda functions, to hundreds of business customers. Last week one customer's misconfigured client flooded the API and degraded service for everyone. The company sells subscription tiers, each with its own request-rate limit and monthly request quota per customer. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a usage plan for each tier with throttling limits and a quota, and give each customer an API key associated with its plan.",
    "Configure stage-level default throttling on the API at the request rate allowed by the lowest subscription tier.",
    "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL to the API stage with a rate-based rule that limits the number of requests coming from each client IP address.",
    "Set reserved concurrency on the backend Lambda functions so that no single client can overwhelm the backend."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Usage plans apply per-API-key rate, burst, and quota limits, so each customer is held to its tier and one noisy client cannot consume everyone's capacity.",
    "Stage throttling applies to all clients combined, so one customer can still use the whole allowance, and it cannot express different tiers or monthly quotas.",
    "Rate-based rules count requests per IP address or other key over short windows; they cannot enforce per-customer monthly quotas or tiered plans.",
    "Reserved concurrency caps total backend concurrency for everyone, so a noisy customer would still crowd out others; it has no notion of customers or quotas."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q09-016", day:9, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway","Caching"],
 q:"A travel site exposes a REST API in Amazon API Gateway that returns hotel availability summaries from an Aurora database. A few thousand city-and-date queries make up most traffic, and results may be up to 5 minutes old. During sales, database CPU spikes and API latency climbs. The team wants the quickest fix that requires no changes to the backend code or its configuration. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Add an Amazon ElastiCache cluster and cache the query results in the backend.",
    "Add Aurora Replicas and send the backend's read queries to the reader endpoint.",
    "Lower the stage throttling limits so that the database is never overloaded.",
    "Enable API Gateway stage caching with a 300-second TTL, keyed on city and date."],
 a:[3],
 x:["ElastiCache would reduce database load, but the backend code must be changed to read and populate the cache.",
    "Replicas add read capacity but require the backend to connect to the reader endpoint, which is a configuration change, and every request still runs a query.",
    "Throttling protects the database by rejecting customer requests during sales, which hurts the business instead of serving the traffic.",
    "The stage cache returns stored responses for repeated city-and-date requests for up to 300 seconds, within the 5-minute staleness allowance, so most requests never reach the backend. It is a stage setting with no backend change."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q09-017", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Containers","Load balancing"],
 q:"A startup is splitting its monolithic web application into four containerized microservices: users, catalog, cart, and checkout. Each service must scale independently on its own load, be deployed by its own team, and be reachable under one public hostname by URL path (for example, /cart/*). The startup has no staff to patch or manage servers. Which combination of steps meets these requirements with the least operational overhead? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Run each microservice as its own Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate with its own service auto scaling policy.",
    "Run all four containers in one ECS task definition so that they are deployed and scaled together.",
    "Create a Network Load Balancer with a listener rule for each URL path that forwards to each service.",
    "Create an Application Load Balancer with path-based listener rules that forward to a target group per service.",
    "Run the services on an ECS cluster backed by an Auto Scaling group of EC2 container instances."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Separate ECS services can be deployed and scaled independently, and Fargate removes the need to provision or patch servers.",
    "Containers in one task definition always scale and deploy as a unit, which defeats independent scaling and team ownership.",
    "Network Load Balancers work at layer 4 and cannot route requests by URL path.",
    "ALB listener rules route requests by path to separate target groups, so all four services share one hostname while scaling independently.",
    "EC2 container instances must be patched and managed, which the startup has no staff to do; Fargate is lower overhead."],
 ref:["d9e2","d1c8"], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q09-018", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS"],
 q:"An online grocery service lets customers cancel an order within 10 minutes of placing it. Each new order is sent to an Amazon SQS standard queue, and a fulfillment worker starts picking the items as soon as it receives the message, so cancelled orders are often already picked. The company wants every order message to become available to the worker only after 10 minutes, with the least change to the producer and the worker. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Set the queue's visibility timeout to 10 minutes so that each order stays hidden until the cancellation window ends.",
    "Configure the queue as a delay queue with a 10-minute (600-second) delivery delay for every new message.",
    "Set the queue's receive message wait time to 10 minutes so that the worker long polls for each order.",
    "Set the queue's message retention period to 10 minutes so that cancelled orders expire before processing."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Visibility timeout hides a message only after a consumer has received it, so the worker would still receive and start each order immediately.",
    "A delay queue keeps every new message invisible for the configured delay, up to 15 minutes, so orders reach the worker only after the cancellation window, with no code changes.",
    "The receive wait time for long polling is capped at 20 seconds and only controls how long a receive call waits for messages; it does not delay delivery.",
    "Retention would delete every order after 10 minutes, including the valid ones, instead of delaying them."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-019", day:9, dom:2, tags:["CloudFront","Caching"],
 q:"A news publisher serves its website from EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. During breaking-news events, traffic grows 30-fold within minutes. Most requests are for the same article pages and images, which are identical for all readers and can be cached for 60 seconds. The Auto Scaling group cannot launch instances fast enough, and the site becomes unavailable. Which solution most effectively absorbs these spikes?",
 o:["Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the ALB to route readers through the nearest edge location.",
    "Add a predictive scaling policy to the Auto Scaling group that is based on past traffic patterns.",
    "Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB and cache pages and images for 60 seconds.",
    "Add an Amazon ElastiCache cluster to cache the database queries used to build article pages."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Global Accelerator proxies packets at the edge to the application over the AWS network, which improves network paths and failover, but every request still reaches the EC2 instances.",
    "Breaking news cannot be predicted from history, so a forecast will not add capacity before an unexpected surge.",
    "CloudFront serves cached copies from edge locations, so the vast majority of these identical requests never reach the origin, and the ALB and instances see only a small fraction of the spike.",
    "Caching database results helps the data tier, but every page request still hits the overloaded web instances."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q09-020", day:9, dom:2, tags:["RDS"],
 q:"A SaaS company runs Amazon RDS for MySQL in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. During the day, business analysts run long, heavy reporting queries that slow down customer transactions. The reports can tolerate data that is a few minutes old. Which solution resolves the performance problem with the least impact on the application?",
 o:["Create a read replica and point the analysts' reporting tool at the replica's endpoint.",
    "Point the analysts' reporting tool at the endpoint of the Multi-AZ standby instance.",
    "Put RDS Proxy in front of the database to pool the reporting tool's connections.",
    "Increase the DB instance's allocated storage to raise the volume's baseline IOPS."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A read replica receives changes asynchronously and serves read-only queries, so reports run on separate capacity and the primary stays free for transactions. A little replica lag is acceptable here.",
    "In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment the standby cannot serve reads; it exists only for failover.",
    "RDS Proxy pools and reuses connections, but the heavy queries still run on the primary and consume its CPU and memory.",
    "More IOPS may help some I/O-bound queries, but reports still compete with transactions for CPU and memory on the same instance."],
 ref:["d9e2","d3c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q09-021", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Aurora","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A social media analytics company uses Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL. Read traffic swings from very low overnight to about 15 times higher during business hours in unpredictable bursts, while write traffic stays steady. The application already sends all read queries to the cluster's reader endpoint. The company wants read capacity to follow demand automatically while keeping costs low. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Move the writer to a larger instance class during business hours with a scheduled job.",
    "Add an Aurora Global Database secondary Region to serve the additional read traffic.",
    "Migrate to an RDS Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment with two readable standby instances.",
    "Configure Aurora Auto Scaling with a target tracking policy for the Aurora Replicas."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Reads already go to the reader endpoint, so a larger writer adds no read capacity, and a schedule cannot follow unpredictable bursts.",
    "A secondary Region is for cross-Region disaster recovery and local reads in another geography; it adds cost and does not scale capacity behind the existing reader endpoint.",
    "That RDS deployment option has one writer and a fixed pair of reader DB instances, so read capacity would not grow or shrink with demand, and it means leaving Aurora.",
    "Aurora Auto Scaling adds replicas when a metric such as average reader CPU or connections rises and removes them when demand drops. New replicas join the reader endpoint automatically, so the application needs no change."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q09-022", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SQS","DynamoDB"],
 q:"A payments company accepts charge requests through an API and queues them in an SQS Standard queue for worker containers. Occasionally a customer is charged twice: SQS can deliver a message more than once, and mobile clients sometimes resend the same charge request hours later after a network timeout. Every request carries a unique client-generated request ID. Which solution prevents duplicate charges?",
 o:["Switch to an SQS FIFO queue and use the request ID as the message deduplication ID.",
    "Have workers store each request ID with a DynamoDB conditional write and skip IDs already seen.",
    "Increase the visibility timeout so that the same message is never received by two workers.",
    "Enable long polling so that workers query all of the SQS servers and duplicate deliveries are avoided."],
 a:[1],
 x:["FIFO deduplication applies only within a 5-minute window, so a resend hours later would still be accepted and charged.",
    "An idempotent consumer is the reliable fix: a conditional put (attribute_not_exists) on the request ID succeeds only once, so any later delivery or resend of the same ID is detected and skipped, however late it arrives.",
    "A longer visibility timeout reduces redeliveries during processing but cannot rule out at-least-once duplicates, and it does nothing about client resends.",
    "Long polling reduces empty and false-empty responses; it does not change the at-least-once delivery of Standard queues."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q09-023", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"A gaming company collects player telemetry events at a high, steady rate. Three independent applications (a real-time leaderboard, a cheat-detection service, and an archival job) must each read every event, in order for each player. After deploying a new model, the cheat-detection team must be able to reprocess the last 24 hours of events. Which service should the company place between the producers and these consumers?",
 o:["Amazon SQS FIFO queue with the player ID as the message group ID",
    "Amazon SQS Standard queue with the maximum message retention period",
    "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the player ID as the partition key",
    "Amazon Data Firehose delivering the events to an Amazon S3 bucket"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Each SQS message is processed by one consumer and then deleted, so three applications cannot each read every event, and processed messages cannot be replayed.",
    "Standard queues lack per-player ordering, each message goes to only one consumer, and deleted messages cannot be reprocessed.",
    "Records with the same partition key land on the same shard in order; several applications read the stream independently (optionally with enhanced fan-out), and a retention period above the 24-hour default (up to 365 days) lets a consumer re-read the last day from an earlier position.",
    "Firehose buffers incoming data and delivers it to destinations such as S3; an archive in S3 is not a stream that three applications can each read in order in real time and replay, so it cannot drive a live leaderboard."],
 ref:["d9e2","d6c3"], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q09-024", day:9, dom:2, tags:["API Gateway","SQS"],
 q:"A ticketing company's purchase API uses Amazon API Gateway to invoke a Lambda function that writes each order directly to a legacy order database. During on-sale events, bursts exceed what the database can absorb and requests fail with timeouts. Customers can receive their order confirmation by email a few minutes later. The company wants to accept every request during bursts and protect the database, with minimal custom code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Integrate API Gateway directly with an SQS queue, and have a Lambda consumer with capped concurrency write to the database.",
    "Increase the Lambda function's timeout and memory so that each request can wait longer for the database to respond.",
    "Enable API Gateway caching on the purchase method so that far fewer requests reach the backend Lambda function.",
    "Configure provisioned concurrency on the Lambda function so that sudden bursts of requests are handled without cold starts."],
 a:[0],
 x:["API Gateway's AWS service integration writes each request to SQS without code and responds quickly. The queue absorbs the burst, and the event source mapping's maximum concurrency limits how fast orders are written to the database.",
    "Longer timeouts keep more concurrent connections open against an already saturated database, making the overload worse.",
    "Purchases are unique writes, not repeatable reads, so caching them would return wrong results instead of reducing load.",
    "More warm concurrency sends even more simultaneous writes to the database, increasing the pressure instead of smoothing it."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"apigw"},

{id:"q09-025", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Lambda","SQS"],
 q:"A retail analytics company uses an SQS queue as the event source for a Lambda function with a batch size of 10. When one message in a batch fails validation, the function throws an error, all 10 messages return to the queue, and the successful ones are processed again, causing duplicate downstream writes. The company wants only failed messages retried while keeping the current batch size. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Reduce the event source mapping's batch size to 1 so that each message is retried entirely on its own.",
    "Configure a dead-letter queue with a maxReceiveCount of 1 on the source queue.",
    "Increase the queue's visibility timeout to six times the function's timeout.",
    "Turn on ReportBatchItemFailures for the event source mapping and return the failed message IDs."],
 a:[3],
 x:["This isolates failures but breaks the requirement to keep the batch size, multiplies invocations, and lowers throughput.",
    "A failed batch would send all 10 messages, including the valid ones, to the DLQ after one attempt, so good messages would go unprocessed.",
    "Six times the function timeout is the recommended visibility timeout for an SQS event source, but it does not change the fact that a failed batch returns every message.",
    "With partial batch responses, the function returns batchItemFailures listing only the failed message IDs; Lambda deletes the successful messages, and only the failed ones become visible again."],
 ref:["d9e2"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q09-026", day:9, dom:2, tags:["High availability design","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A company's customer portal runs on two EC2 instances in a single Availability Zone behind an Application Load Balancer, with an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL Single-AZ DB instance in the same AZ. An outage in that AZ recently took the portal offline for several hours. The portal must now survive the loss of an Availability Zone with automatic recovery and no manual intervention. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Replace the two instances with a single larger instance type that has more capacity.",
    "Run the web instances in an Auto Scaling group that spans at least two AZs, registered with the ALB.",
    "Modify the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment with a synchronous standby in another AZ.",
    "Create a read replica of the DB instance in a second AZ to take over if the primary fails.",
    "Copy the automated DB snapshots to a second AWS Region once a day."],
 a:[1,2],
 x:["One instance in one AZ is still a single point of failure, so an AZ outage would take the portal down again.",
    "Instances spread across AZs keep serving through the ALB if one AZ fails, and the Auto Scaling group replaces lost capacity automatically in the healthy AZs.",
    "Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically by updating the endpoint's DNS record, without data loss or manual steps.",
    "Promoting a read replica is a manual action and replication is asynchronous, so it does not give automatic failover.",
    "Daily cross-Region snapshots help disaster recovery but need a manual restore and can lose up to a day of data; they do not provide automatic AZ failover."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q09-027", day:9, dom:2, tags:["EC2 instance types","High availability design"],
 q:"A company runs a legacy clustered trading engine on four large EC2 instances in a single Availability Zone, because the vendor supports only one AZ. The cluster tolerates the loss of one node but not two at the same time. The company wants to reduce the risk that a single hardware failure, such as a rack power or network fault, takes down more than one node. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Launch the four instances in a cluster placement group so that they run close together for low-latency networking.",
    "Launch the four instances in a partition placement group with all of them placed in the same partition.",
    "Launch the four instances in a spread placement group so that each instance runs on distinct hardware.",
    "Launch the four instances as Dedicated Instances so that no other customer's instances share their hosts."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A cluster placement group packs instances close together for low latency, which increases the chance that one hardware failure affects several nodes.",
    "Instances in the same partition share racks, so a single rack failure could take down all of them.",
    "A spread placement group places each instance on a distinct rack with its own network and power source, so one hardware failure affects at most one node; it suits small groups of critical instances.",
    "Dedicated Instances isolate the hardware from other customers but do not spread the company's own instances across separate racks."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"placement"},

{id:"q09-028", day:9, dom:2, tags:["RDS","High availability design"],
 q:"A fintech company runs Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL in a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. Failovers during maintenance take one to two minutes, which is too long for its trading application. The company also wants to offload some read-only queries without creating and managing separate read replicas, and it wants to stay on RDS for PostgreSQL. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Move the database to a Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment with two readable standby instances.",
    "Keep the Multi-AZ DB instance deployment and add two read replicas in other Availability Zones.",
    "Place RDS Proxy in front of the existing Multi-AZ DB instance deployment and change nothing else.",
    "Migrate the database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with two Aurora Replicas in other AZs."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A Multi-AZ DB cluster has a writer and two readable standbys (the user guide calls them reader DB instances) in three AZs, typically fails over in under 35 seconds, and offers a reader endpoint for read-only queries, all within RDS for PostgreSQL.",
    "Read replicas are the separate replicas the company does not want to manage, and they do not shorten Multi-AZ failover time.",
    "RDS Proxy can soften the impact of failover on application connections, but it adds no readable capacity for the read-only queries.",
    "Aurora would provide fast failover and readers, but it means migrating to a different database engine, which the company wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d9e3","d3c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q09-029", day:9, dom:2, tags:["SNS","SQS"],
 q:"An Amazon SNS standard topic sends order events to several SQS queues and to a partner's HTTPS endpoint. The partner can receive events only as HTTPS pushes. During a partner outage that lasted several hours, SNS exhausted its delivery retries and the partner's events were lost. The company wants undeliverable partner events to be kept so that they can be delivered after an outage, with the least custom work. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Raise the HTTPS delivery retry policy on the subscription to its maximum number of retries and backoff.",
    "Attach an SQS dead-letter queue to the partner's HTTPS subscription by setting its redrive policy.",
    "Enable delivery status logging for HTTPS on the topic so that failed deliveries are written to CloudWatch Logs.",
    "Replace the HTTPS subscription with a Lambda function that forwards events and stores failures in DynamoDB."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Retry policies extend delivery attempts for a limited time only, so an outage longer than the retry window would still lose events.",
    "A subscription dead-letter queue captures messages that SNS cannot deliver after its retries, so the partner's events are kept in SQS and can be sent again once the partner recovers.",
    "Delivery status logs record whether deliveries succeeded; they do not keep the messages for redelivery.",
    "Custom forwarding code with its own failure storage and redelivery logic is much more work than the built-in subscription dead-letter queue."],
 ref:["d9e2","d9c1"], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q09-030", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Aurora","High availability design"],
 q:"A media company runs an Aurora MySQL DB cluster that has only a writer instance. Although the cluster storage is replicated across three Availability Zones, when the writer instance failed last month the database was unavailable for roughly 10 minutes while Aurora created a replacement instance. The company wants future instance failures to be handled by a fast failover instead. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Turn on Aurora Backtrack for the DB cluster.",
    "Add an Aurora Global Database secondary Region to the cluster.",
    "Increase automated backup retention to 35 days.",
    "Add an Aurora Replica in a different Availability Zone."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Backtrack rewinds the cluster to an earlier point in time to undo bad changes; it does not provide an instance to fail over to.",
    "A secondary Region protects against Regional outages, but the primary cluster would still have no in-Region replica, so an instance failure would still wait for a new instance.",
    "Longer backup retention extends point-in-time recovery; it does nothing to speed up recovery from an instance failure.",
    "With an Aurora Replica, Aurora promotes the replica to writer and repoints the cluster endpoint, which is much faster than creating a new instance; placing it in another AZ also covers an AZ failure."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q09-031", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Aurora","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A global e-commerce company runs Aurora PostgreSQL in eu-west-1. It needs disaster recovery in us-east-1 with an RPO of about 1 second and an RTO of about 1 minute for a Regional outage. During normal operations, it also wants customers in North America to get low-latency reads from us-east-1. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Copy Aurora snapshots to us-east-1 every hour by using AWS Backup.",
    "Add Aurora Replicas in more Availability Zones within eu-west-1.",
    "Convert the cluster to an Aurora Global Database with a secondary cluster in us-east-1.",
    "Use AWS DMS to replicate continuously to an RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance in us-east-1."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Hourly snapshots give an RPO of up to an hour, a restore takes far longer than a minute, and they provide no reads in us-east-1.",
    "More replicas in eu-west-1 improve in-Region availability but give no protection from a Regional outage and no local reads for North America.",
    "Aurora Global Database replicates at the storage layer with typical lag under a second, the secondary cluster serves local reads, and it can be promoted to take writes in less than a minute.",
    "DMS replication lag is usually higher and less predictable, failover is manual, and the company would manage a separate database and its replication tasks."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c4","d9c2"], doc:"aurora"},

{id:"q09-032", day:9, dom:2, tags:["DynamoDB","High availability design"],
 q:"A mobile gaming company stores player profiles and inventories in an Amazon DynamoDB table in one Region. It is launching in Europe and Asia and wants players to read and write their data with single-digit-millisecond latency in a nearby Region. If one Region becomes unavailable, the game must keep accepting writes in the other Regions. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Convert the table to a DynamoDB global table with replicas in Regions in Europe and Asia.",
    "Deploy DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) clusters in Europe and Asia in front of the existing table.",
    "Use DynamoDB Streams and Lambda functions to copy each change to tables in the other Regions.",
    "Export the table to Amazon S3 every hour and import it into new tables in Europe and Asia."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Global tables are fully managed multi-active replicas: every replica accepts local reads and writes, changes replicate to the other Regions, and the remaining Regions keep working if one fails.",
    "DAX is an in-memory cache that runs in a VPC and speeds up eventually consistent reads; it does not replicate the table, so every write would still depend on the single Region that holds the table.",
    "This custom replication can work, but the company would have to build, operate, and resolve conflicts in it, which global tables handle automatically.",
    "Hourly exports produce stale copies and offer no way to keep writes in sync across Regions."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c4"], doc:"global-tables"},

{id:"q09-033", day:9, dom:2, tags:["S3 features","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A financial services firm must keep a copy of every new trade-confirmation object in an S3 bucket in a second AWS Region. A regulator requires a predictable replication time of 15 minutes or less for new objects, backed by a service level agreement, and the firm must be alerted when replication falls behind. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable S3 Versioning on both the source and the destination buckets.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the destination bucket.",
    "Schedule an AWS DataSync task every 15 minutes to copy new objects to the second Region.",
    "Configure Same-Region Replication with replication metrics enabled.",
    "Configure a Cross-Region Replication rule with S3 Replication Time Control enabled."],
 a:[0,4],
 x:["S3 replication requires versioning on both the source and destination buckets, so this is a prerequisite for any replication rule.",
    "Transfer Acceleration speeds up client uploads over long distances; it does not replicate objects or offer a replication SLA.",
    "A scheduled copy job has no replication SLA or per-object tracking, and an object written just after a run would wait up to 15 minutes plus copy time.",
    "Same-Region Replication keeps the copy in the same Region, which fails the second-Region requirement.",
    "Replication Time Control is designed to replicate 99.99% of new objects within 15 minutes (its SLA commits to 99.9%), and it turns on replication metrics and event notifications for objects that miss the threshold."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q09-034", day:9, dom:2, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"A media company set up Cross-Region Replication on a bucket that already held 200 TB of video assets. A week later, an audit finds that only objects uploaded after the rule was created exist in the destination bucket, and some recent objects show a replication status of FAILED. The company needs the existing and failed objects replicated with the least operational effort. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Edit the replication rule to enable S3 Replication Time Control.",
    "Create an S3 Batch Replication job for the existing and failed objects.",
    "Run <code>aws s3 sync</code> from an EC2 instance to copy objects to the destination.",
    "Suspend and then re-enable versioning on the source bucket."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Replication Time Control adds an SLA and metrics for new objects; it does not replicate objects that existed before the rule or that already failed.",
    "Live replication applies only to new objects. S3 Batch Replication is the managed way to replicate existing objects and objects whose earlier replication failed, using the existing rule.",
    "A sync script must be run, monitored, and retried by the company, and it copies only current versions as new objects instead of replicating versions and metadata.",
    "Replication needs versioning to stay enabled, and toggling it off and on does not cause existing or failed objects to be replicated."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q09-035", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery","AWS Backup"],
 q:"A small accounting firm runs an internal document management system on two EC2 instances with EBS volumes and an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The system is not customer facing: the firm can tolerate losing up to 24 hours of data and taking up to 48 hours to recover in another Region after a Regional disaster. The firm wants the lowest-cost disaster recovery strategy, with centrally managed, policy-based backups. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["A pilot light environment with a cross-Region read replica and AMIs ready to launch",
    "A warm standby that runs a scaled-down copy of the system in a second Region",
    "A multi-site active/active deployment in two Regions behind Amazon Route 53",
    "An AWS Backup plan that takes daily backups and copies them to a second Region"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Pilot light keeps a database replica running at all times, which costs more than needed when 24 hours of data loss and 48 hours of downtime are acceptable.",
    "A warm standby runs servers and a database around the clock in the DR Region, far more than these relaxed targets require.",
    "Active/active runs full infrastructure in two Regions for near-zero RTO and RPO; it is the most expensive strategy and unnecessary here.",
    "Backup and restore is the lowest-cost, least complex strategy and fits an RPO and RTO measured in hours. AWS Backup centrally schedules EC2, EBS, and RDS backups and copies recovery points to another Region automatically."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q09-036", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery"],
 q:"An online retailer needs a disaster recovery strategy in a second Region for its order-management system. The RPO is under 5 minutes and the RTO is about 1 hour. Data must be replicated continuously, but the company wants to pay as little as possible for compute in the DR Region until a disaster occurs. Which strategy should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Back up the servers and the database every 4 hours with AWS Backup and copy the backups to the second Region.",
    "Replicate the database continuously to the second Region and keep app server AMIs and templates ready to launch.",
    "Run a scaled-down but fully functional copy of the entire stack in the second Region at all times.",
    "Run the full production stack in both Regions and split traffic between them by using Route 53 weighted routing."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Backups every 4 hours mean up to 4 hours of data loss, which misses the 5-minute RPO.",
    "This is pilot light: only the core data layer runs and replicates continuously, and application servers are launched from AMIs and templates during a disaster, which fits the one-hour RTO and keeps the ongoing cost of the DR Region to a minimum.",
    "This warm standby would meet the targets but keeps application servers running all the time, which costs more than the company wants.",
    "Active/active meets far stricter targets but runs full capacity in both Regions, the most expensive option."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"dr"},

{id:"q09-037", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A B2B logistics platform must recover from a Regional outage within 10 minutes with less than 1 minute of data loss. Leadership accepts paying for always-on infrastructure in the DR Region, but not for full production capacity there. The DR environment must be able to serve some traffic immediately while it scales up. Which disaster recovery strategy meets these requirements?",
 o:["Backup and restore, with frequent cross-Region backup copies",
    "Warm standby, with a scaled-down but working copy of production",
    "Pilot light, with data replicated and application servers switched off",
    "Multi-site active/active, with full capacity in both Regions"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Backup and restore means redeploying the infrastructure and restoring data from periodic backups, so neither the 10-minute RTO nor the 1-minute RPO can be met.",
    "A warm standby runs every component at reduced size with continuous data replication, so it can take traffic immediately at reduced capacity and then scale out, which a 10-minute RTO needs, without paying for full capacity.",
    "Pilot light cannot process requests until its servers are turned on, any remaining infrastructure is deployed and the stack is scaled up, so it serves no traffic immediately and puts a 10-minute RTO at risk.",
    "Active/active meets the targets but means paying for full production capacity in both Regions, which leadership rejected."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"dr"},

{id:"q09-038", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery","DynamoDB"],
 q:"A global payments-tracking application must stay available with near-zero downtime and near-zero data loss even if an entire AWS Region fails. Users in North America and Europe should be served by the Region nearest to them, and both Regions must accept writes at all times. The application tier is stateless and already runs in both Regions. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Use Route 53 failover routing with the European Region as primary and North America as secondary.",
    "Store application data in Amazon DynamoDB global tables with replicas in both Regions.",
    "Use an Aurora global database with the writer cluster in the North American Region.",
    "Use RDS for MySQL with a cross-Region read replica in the European Region.",
    "Use Route 53 latency-based routing with health checks on each Region's endpoint."],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Failover routing is active/passive: all users would go to Europe while it is healthy, so North American users would not be served locally.",
    "Global tables accept writes in every replica Region and replicate changes between them, typically within a second, so either Region can serve reads and writes and keep running if the other fails.",
    "An Aurora global database has a single writer Region; even with write forwarding, writes depend on it, so if it fails, writes stop until a secondary is promoted.",
    "A read replica is read-only and its promotion is manual, so Europe cannot accept writes and failover is not near-zero downtime.",
    "Latency-based routing sends each user to the Region with the lowest latency, and health checks remove a failed Region from answers so all traffic moves to the healthy one."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2","d9c4"], doc:"global-tables"},

{id:"q09-039", day:9, dom:2, tags:["CloudFront","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A publisher serves images and static assets through Amazon CloudFront from an S3 bucket in eu-west-1, and the bucket is copied to a second bucket in eu-central-1 with S3 Cross-Region Replication. If the eu-west-1 bucket becomes unavailable or returns server errors, viewers must automatically receive the content from the replica, without any DNS change or manual action. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a CloudFront origin group: the eu-west-1 bucket as primary, the replica as secondary, failover on 5xx errors.",
    "Create Route 53 failover records for the two buckets' website endpoints and use that record as the distribution's origin.",
    "Create a second CloudFront distribution for the replica bucket and switch the site's alias record to it during an outage.",
    "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on both buckets and add the replica's accelerate endpoint as a second origin domain."],
 a:[0],
 x:["With an origin group, CloudFront automatically retries a request against the secondary origin when the primary returns one of the configured error codes or cannot be reached, with no DNS change.",
    "This depends on DNS health checks and caching rather than per-request failover, and an S3 website endpoint answers only for a host name that matches its bucket name, so one record cannot front both buckets.",
    "Switching distributions requires a manual DNS change, which the requirement rules out.",
    "Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers to a bucket; it provides no automatic failover between origins."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q09-040", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Route 53"],
 q:"A video streaming company has deployed version 2 of its API on a separate Application Load Balancer and Auto Scaling group in the same Region as version 1. It wants Route 53 to send 5% of all requests to version 2 regardless of where users are, watch error rates, and then raise the share step by step to 100%, with the option to shift traffic back quickly. Which routing policy should the company use?",
 o:["Failover routing, with version 1 as primary and version 2 as secondary",
    "Latency routing, with one record for each version's load balancer",
    "Geoproximity routing, with a small bias on version 2's load balancer",
    "Weighted routing, with weights of 95 and 5 that are adjusted over time"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Failover sends traffic to the secondary only when the primary is unhealthy, so it cannot send a controlled 5% share.",
    "Latency routing picks the lowest-latency record for each user; with both stacks in the same Region, it cannot split traffic by percentage.",
    "Geoproximity shifts traffic based on users' geographic location and a bias, not a fixed percentage of all users regardless of location.",
    "Weighted routing answers with each record in proportion to its weight, so moving the weights from 95/5 toward 0/100, or back, controls the canary share. A low TTL makes changes take effect quickly."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c3"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q09-041", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Route 53"],
 q:"A streaming service's licensing agreements require that users in Germany and France are served only from infrastructure in eu-central-1, while users everywhere else are served from us-east-1. DNS queries from IP addresses whose location cannot be determined must still receive an answer. Which Route 53 configuration meets these requirements?",
 o:["Latency records for the eu-central-1 and us-east-1 endpoints.",
    "Geoproximity records with a positive bias on the eu-central-1 endpoint.",
    "Geolocation records for Germany and France pointing to eu-central-1, plus a default record pointing to us-east-1.",
    "Geolocation records for Germany and France pointing to eu-central-1, plus a United States record pointing to us-east-1."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Latency routing follows measured network latency, which can send German or French users to us-east-1, so the licensing rule cannot be guaranteed.",
    "Geoproximity routes by distance and bias, not by country borders, so it cannot guarantee that only German and French users reach eu-central-1.",
    "Geolocation maps users by country, and the default record answers every query that matches no country, including IP addresses whose location is unknown.",
    "Users outside Germany, France, and the United States, and unlocatable IP addresses, would match no record and receive no answer."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c3"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q09-042", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Route 53"],
 q:"A company serves users across North America from us-east-1 and us-west-2, and Route 53 currently sends each user to the geographically closer Region. After adding capacity in us-west-2, the company wants that Region to take a larger share of users from the central United States. It does not want to assign traffic by individual country or state, or by fixed percentages. Which routing approach meets this requirement?",
 o:["Weighted routing with a higher weight on us-west-2",
    "Geoproximity routing with a positive bias on us-west-2",
    "Geolocation routing with central US states mapped to us-west-2",
    "Latency routing with health checks on both Regions"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Weights split traffic by fixed percentages across all users regardless of location, which the company does not want.",
    "Geoproximity routes users to the nearest resource by geographic distance, and a positive bias enlarges the area served by us-west-2 so it draws more users from the middle of the country.",
    "This assigns traffic by individual state, which the company explicitly wants to avoid.",
    "Latency routing follows measured network latency and offers no control to enlarge one Region's share."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c3"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q09-043", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Route 53"],
 q:"An IoT company runs a UDP-based ingestion service on eight EC2 instances, each with an Elastic IP address, in one Region. Clients resolve a DNS name and pick one of the returned IP addresses. The company wants DNS responses to include only healthy instances and to return several addresses per query to spread load, without paying for a load balancer. Which Route 53 configuration meets these requirements?",
 o:["Multivalue answer records, one per instance, each associated with a health check.",
    "A single simple routing record that lists all eight Elastic IP addresses.",
    "Weighted records with equal weights, one per instance, each associated with a health check.",
    "Failover records with four instances as primary and four as secondary."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Multivalue answer routing returns up to eight healthy records per query and leaves out any record whose health check fails, giving simple DNS-level load spreading.",
    "A simple record can list all eight addresses, but with simple routing the values are not health checked, so clients would still receive the addresses of failed instances.",
    "Weighted routing returns only one record per query, so clients would not receive several addresses to choose from.",
    "Failover routing is active/passive: the secondary addresses get no traffic while the primary is healthy, and health is judged per record, not per instance."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c3"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q09-044", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Route 53","CloudWatch"],
 q:"A company uses Route 53 failover routing between a primary application in its VPC and a standby in another Region. The primary is healthy only if an internal service in private subnets is healthy. That service has no public endpoint, and the security team will not allow Route 53 health checkers into the VPC. How should the company configure the health check for the primary record?",
 o:["Create an endpoint health check that targets the internal service's private IP address.",
    "Create a calculated health check that combines the health of the internal service's instances.",
    "Give the internal service a public IP address and allow the Route 53 health checker IP ranges.",
    "Create a health check that monitors a CloudWatch alarm on the internal service's health metric."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Route 53 health checkers run on the internet and cannot reach private IP addresses inside a VPC.",
    "A calculated health check only combines other health checks; the child checks would still need to reach the private service.",
    "This exposes the internal service publicly and lets health checkers in, which the security team prohibits.",
    "A Route 53 health check can take its status from a CloudWatch alarm, so a metric published from inside the VPC (for example, a healthy host count) drives failover without any inbound access."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c3"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q09-045", day:9, dom:2, tags:["AWS Backup","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A healthcare company uses AWS Organizations. After a ransomware attack at a peer company, it wants backups of its production EC2 instances, EBS volumes, and Amazon EFS file systems to survive a compromise of the production account or the loss of the primary Region. Backup copies must be kept in an account that production administrators cannot access, and must not be deletable by anyone, including the root user, before their 1-year retention ends. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in governance mode to the destination vault.",
    "Copy recovery points to a second Region within the production account.",
    "Add a copy rule that copies recovery points to a vault in a dedicated backup account in another Region.",
    "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the destination vault.",
    "Store the backups in a versioned S3 bucket in the production account."],
 a:[2,3],
 x:["Governance mode can be changed or removed by users with sufficient IAM permissions, so a compromised administrator could still delete backups.",
    "This protects against losing the Region, but the copies stay in the production account, where its administrators (and anyone holding their credentials) still have access, which breaks the isolation requirement.",
    "A cross-account, cross-Region copy puts recovery points outside the production account's control and outside the primary Region; AWS Organizations enables cross-account backup between its accounts.",
    "Once the grace period ends, compliance mode makes the lock immutable: no user, including the root user, can delete recovery points before retention ends or remove the lock.",
    "The bucket stays in the production account, so its administrators could still delete object versions, and AWS Backup keeps the recovery points for EC2, EBS, and EFS in backup vaults, which is where Vault Lock applies."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q09-046", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Disaster recovery","Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A manufacturing company runs 30 business-critical Windows and Linux servers on VMware in its on-premises data center. It wants AWS as its disaster recovery site with an RPO of seconds and an RTO of minutes, while paying as little as possible for idle compute until a disaster or a drill. The applications must not be rewritten or re-architected. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Back up the VMware virtual machines nightly with AWS Backup and restore them in AWS during a disaster.",
    "Use AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery to replicate the servers continuously to a low-cost staging area in AWS.",
    "Use AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway snapshots and restore them as EBS volumes during a disaster.",
    "Use AWS DMS to replicate each server's data continuously to Amazon RDS databases in the DR Region."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Nightly backups give an RPO of up to 24 hours, and restoring whole machines takes much longer than minutes.",
    "Elastic Disaster Recovery performs continuous block-level replication (RPO of seconds) into a staging area of lightweight resources and launches full recovery instances only for a drill or disaster (RTO of minutes).",
    "Volume Gateway snapshots are point-in-time copies taken on a schedule of every 1 to 24 hours, so the RPO is an hour at best, and they protect iSCSI volumes, not whole servers, which would have to be rebuilt around the restored volumes.",
    "DMS migrates and replicates databases, not whole servers, so the applications and operating systems would not be recoverable."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"drs"},

{id:"q09-047", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Internet & NAT gateways","High availability design"],
 q:"A company runs application servers in private subnets in two Availability Zones. The servers call third-party payment APIs over the internet through one NAT gateway in the first AZ, which both private route tables use. During an impairment of the first AZ, servers in the second AZ were healthy but could not reach the payment APIs. Which change removes this single point of failure?",
 o:["Add a NAT gateway in the second AZ's public subnet and point that AZ's private route table at it.",
    "Associate a secondary Elastic IP address with the existing NAT gateway to provide redundancy.",
    "Add a default route to the internet gateway in the route tables of both of the private subnets in the VPC.",
    "Replace the NAT gateway with a NAT instance in an Auto Scaling group in the first AZ."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A zonal NAT gateway is redundant only within its own AZ. One NAT gateway per AZ, with each private route table pointing at the NAT gateway in the same AZ, keeps outbound access working when another AZ fails and avoids cross-AZ traffic (a regional NAT gateway, which spans AZs automatically, is a newer alternative).",
    "Extra IP addresses add source addresses for connections, but the gateway still lives in the first AZ and fails with it.",
    "A route to the internet gateway makes the subnets public; the servers would need public IP addresses and would be exposed, which defeats the purpose of private subnets.",
    "A NAT instance in the same AZ is still a single-AZ dependency and adds patching and scaling work."],
 ref:["d9e3","d2b1"], doc:"nat"},

{id:"q09-048", day:9, dom:2, tags:["EFS","Disaster recovery"],
 q:"A company's document processing application runs on EC2 instances in us-east-1 and keeps its working files on an Amazon EFS file system. The disaster recovery plan requires a copy of the file system in us-west-2 with an RPO of 1 hour, and the operations team wants no scripts or servers to maintain for the copy. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Mount the file system on an EC2 instance in us-west-2 and run an rsync job every hour from cron.",
    "Create an AWS Backup plan that backs up the file system every 24 hours and copies the backups to us-west-2.",
    "Turn on EFS replication to keep a read-only replica file system continuously updated in us-west-2.",
    "Change the file system to EFS One Zone and add a mount target in an Availability Zone in us-west-2."],
 a:[2],
 x:["An rsync job is a script and a server to maintain, and copying a large file system across Regions every hour may not even finish in time.",
    "Daily backups give an RPO of up to 24 hours, which misses the 1-hour target.",
    "EFS replication keeps a read-only replica file system in another Region in sync and maintains an RPO of 15 minutes for most file systems, with no infrastructure to manage; deleting the replication configuration makes the replica writable for failover.",
    "A One Zone file system stores its data in a single Availability Zone and can have only one mount target, in that same zone, so this adds no copy of the data in us-west-2 and lowers resilience."],
 ref:["d9e3","d9c2"], doc:"efs"},

{id:"q09-049", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Lambda","High availability design"],
 q:"A Lambda function that is connected to a VPC writes orders to an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ DB instance. The function was configured with a single private subnet in one Availability Zone. During an impairment of that AZ, the database failed over to its standby in another AZ, but every invocation of the function failed until the AZ recovered. How should a solutions architect make the function resilient to the loss of an AZ?",
 o:["Configure provisioned concurrency on the function so that warm execution environments are always ready.",
    "Place Amazon RDS Proxy in front of the database and point the function's connections at the proxy endpoint.",
    "Set reserved concurrency on the function so that it always has capacity during an AZ impairment.",
    "Configure the function with private subnets in multiple Availability Zones, such as one in each AZ."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Provisioned environments still run in the function's only subnet and AZ, so they fail together with that AZ.",
    "RDS Proxy is highly available and shortens database failover, but the function itself can still run only in the impaired AZ.",
    "Reserved concurrency guarantees how much concurrency the function can use, not where it runs; it still has only one AZ.",
    "Lambda runs a VPC-connected function in the subnets configured for it, so choosing subnets in several AZs lets it keep running when one AZ is impaired."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"lambda"},

{id:"q09-050", day:9, dom:2, tags:["Global Accelerator","High availability design"],
 q:"A multiplayer gaming company runs game servers behind Network Load Balancers in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. Some player devices and ISPs cache DNS answers far longer than their TTL, so DNS-based failover between Regions has taken many minutes in past incidents. Corporate partners also need a small, fixed set of IP addresses to allowlist. The company needs Regional failover within seconds for TCP and UDP traffic. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Route 53 failover routing with a 10-second TTL and health checks on both NLBs.",
    "AWS Global Accelerator with an endpoint group for each Region's NLB.",
    "An Amazon CloudFront distribution with both NLBs in an origin group.",
    "Elastic IP addresses on both NLBs, returned by Route 53 multivalue answer records."],
 a:[1],
 x:["A short TTL does not help when devices and resolvers ignore it, and DNS failover does not give partners one small, fixed IP set that works for both Regions.",
    "Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IP addresses and shifts TCP and UDP traffic between endpoint groups based on health checks, without depending on DNS caches.",
    "CloudFront carries HTTP and HTTPS (including WebSocket) traffic, not arbitrary TCP and UDP game protocols, so it cannot front these game servers.",
    "Clients still choose among cached DNS answers, so failover remains subject to DNS caching, and partners would need to allowlist addresses for every NLB in both Regions."],
 ref:["d9e3"], doc:"ga"}
);
